{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem was in `insert_index` in `repo/search.py`. It behaved like **bisect left**: when `sorted_list[mid] == x`, the search kept shrinking toward the left, so new values with the same key were placed **before** existing ones.\n\nFor a stable “append after equals” index you want the **right** insertion point: treat `mid` as still on the left side when `sorted_list[mid] <= x`, so duplicates land after the run of equal keys.\n\n```4:12:repo/search.py\ndef insert_index(sorted_list, x):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(sorted_list)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if sorted_list[mid] <= x:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n```\n\nExample: for `[1, 2, 2, 3]` and `x = 2`, the index is now `3` (after both `2`s), not `1`. `bisect_summary` in `rows.py` picks this up via `insert_index`. All visible tests pass."}]},"session_id":"59e41074-3cff-411a-9910-ede2c84de3aa"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":53890,"duration_api_ms":53890,"is_error":false,"result":"Searching the codebase for how the sorted index handles duplicate keys.\n\n\nBug is documented: `insert_index` uses left bisection; tests expect inserting after equal elements. Running tests and applying the fix.\n\n\nThe problem was in `insert_index` in `repo/search.py`. It behaved like **bisect left**: when `sorted_list[mid] == x`, the search kept shrinking toward the left, so new values with the same key were placed **before** existing ones.\n\nFor a stable “append after equals” index you want the **right** insertion point: treat `mid` as still on the left side when `sorted_list[mid] <= x`, so duplicates land after the run of equal keys.\n\n```4:12:repo/search.py\ndef insert_index(sorted_list, x):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(sorted_list)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if sorted_list[mid] <= x:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n```\n\nExample: for `[1, 2, 2, 3]` and `x = 2`, the index is now `3` (after both `2`s), not `1`. `bisect_summary` in `rows.py` picks this up via `insert_index`. All visible tests pass.","session_id":"59e41074-3cff-411a-9910-ede2c84de3aa","request_id":"139052de-7791-4407-b77a-bc3aadb53edf","usage":{"inputTokens":12347,"outputTokens":1442,"cacheReadTokens":78393,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
